#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0e53bd-8745-5da4-abb6-0168c5f3b7d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392268} {:answer/id #uuid "476bc4f8-15ca-5f43-b421-6be5db9f13ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392269} {:answer/id #uuid "1e73dd85-d5f3-51a5-becd-e240b1df303b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392270} {:answer/id #uuid "67c66a85-607a-5c01-b5d7-7cb5992959b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392271} {:answer/id #uuid "2b845846-0d25-558e-832d-645c178ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67c66a85-607a-5c01-b5d7-7cb5992959b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392271} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d04520-0124-5203-a746-6e09d2550e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f1bb626-14db-51ca-a15c-04bb2468d62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39124" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f96ed3-70f8-5ddc-acb6-71587e72f101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324543} {:answer/id #uuid "71e88fc2-600f-5365-8aff-acc2b54dc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324914} {:answer/id #uuid "90374c3c-03f1-5264-a569-174dfb35f325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325196} {:answer/id #uuid "aa418aef-76d7-5108-ab92-460aa075c2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325420} {:answer/id #uuid "f69e55ee-410c-52db-978f-37052ef96cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46f96ed3-70f8-5ddc-acb6-71587e72f101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324543} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c656fc-b22b-5e23-846d-595499735480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53d94693-fca2-5635-a557-72030e989666" :question/math-academy-id "q-39161" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1-6\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096e7527-4c01-5dce-8ff8-e0db3c75370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410832} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8fe61d-12cc-5e71-bf5f-8ade179fac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410834} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87451a-2d1c-5373-8af0-e4594174b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410836} {:answer/id #uuid "5113c383-2506-5331-9225-ebb1f5af24a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410840} {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8400b796-2f04-536c-986d-13f15ae5b122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6880ebc-9daa-5cd0-b5d7-2100fa039d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-99345" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
